@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Functional Interfaces in Java";
}

<div class="card border-0 shadow-sm">
    <div class="card-body p-4 p-lg-5">

        <!-- HEADER -->
        <div class="mb-4">
            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">Functional Interfaces in Java</h2>

            <p class="lead text-muted mb-0">
                Learn functional interfaces, the
                <code>@@FunctionalInterface</code> annotation,
                built-in interfaces from <code>java.util.function</code>,
                lambda expressions, method references, and functional-style
                programming in Java.
            </p>
        </div>


        <!-- INTRODUCTION -->
        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">What is a Functional Interface?</h3>

            <p>
                A functional interface is an interface that contains exactly
                one abstract method.
            </p>

            <p>
                Functional interfaces are the target types for lambda
                expressions and method references.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>interface Calculator {

    int calculate(int a, int b);

}</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="alert alert-info mt-3">
                <strong>Important:</strong>
                A functional interface can also contain default and static
                methods. The important requirement is that it has only one
                abstract method.
            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- FUNCTIONAL INTERFACE ANNOTATION -->
        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                @@FunctionalInterface Annotation
            </h3>

            <p>
                The <code>@@FunctionalInterface</code> annotation tells the
                compiler and other developers that an interface is intended
                to be a functional interface.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>@@FunctionalInterface
interface Calculator {

    int calculate(int a, int b);

}</code></pre>
            </div>

            <p class="mt-3">
                If another abstract method is added, the compiler reports an
                error because the interface no longer satisfies the functional
                interface contract.
            </p>

        </section>


        <!-- LAMBDA -->
        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Functional Interface with Lambda
            </h3>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>@@FunctionalInterface
interface Calculator {

    int calculate(int a, int b);

}

Calculator addition =
    (a, b) -&gt; a + b;

int result =
    addition.calculate(10, 20);

System.out.println(result);</code></pre>
            </div>

<p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>30</code></pre>
</div>

            <p class="mt-3">
                The lambda expression provides the implementation of the
                interface's single abstract method.
            </p>

        </section>


        <!-- BUILT-IN INTERFACES -->
        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Built-in Functional Interfaces
            </h3>

            <p>
                Java provides commonly used functional interfaces in the
                <code>java.util.function</code> package.
            </p>

            <div class="table-responsive">

                <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                    <thead class="table-dark">

                        <tr>
                            <th>Interface</th>
                            <th>Input</th>
                            <th>Output</th>
                            <th>Main Method</th>
                        </tr>

                    </thead>

                    <tbody>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>Predicate&lt;T&gt;</code></td>
                            <td>T</td>
                            <td>boolean</td>
                            <td><code>test()</code></td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>Function&lt;T,R&gt;</code></td>
                            <td>T</td>
                            <td>R</td>
                            <td><code>apply()</code></td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>Consumer&lt;T&gt;</code></td>
                            <td>T</td>
                            <td>void</td>
                            <td><code>accept()</code></td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>Supplier&lt;T&gt;</code></td>
                            <td>None</td>
                            <td>T</td>
                            <td><code>get()</code></td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>UnaryOperator&lt;T&gt;</code></td>
                            <td>T</td>
                            <td>T</td>
                            <td><code>apply()</code></td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>BinaryOperator&lt;T&gt;</code></td>
                            <td>T, T</td>
                            <td>T</td>
                            <td><code>apply()</code></td>
                        </tr>

                    </tbody>

                </table>

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- PREDICATE -->
        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Predicate&lt;T&gt;
            </h3>

            <p>
                Predicate represents a condition that returns either
                <code>true</code> or <code>false</code>.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>import java.util.function.Predicate;

Predicate&lt;Integer&gt; isPositive =
    number -&gt; number &gt; 0;

System.out.println(
    isPositive.test(10)
);

System.out.println(
    isPositive.test(-5)
);</code></pre>
            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>true
 false</code></pre>
</div>
        </section>


        <!-- FUNCTION -->
        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Function&lt;T,R&gt;
            </h3>

            <p>
                Function accepts one input and produces one output.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>import java.util.function.Function;

Function&lt;String, Integer&gt; length =
    text -&gt; text.length();

int result =
    length.apply("CIIT Institute");

System.out.println(result);</code></pre>
            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>14</code></pre>
</div>
        </section>


        <!-- CONSUMER -->
        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Consumer&lt;T&gt;
            </h3>

            <p>
                Consumer accepts an input but does not return a value.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>import java.util.function.Consumer;

Consumer&lt;String&gt; printer =
    text -&gt; System.out.println(text);

printer.accept(
    "Welcome to CIIT Institute"
);</code></pre>
            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>Welcome to CIIT Institute
</code></pre>
</div>
        </section>


        <!-- SUPPLIER -->
        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Supplier&lt;T&gt;
            </h3>

            <p>
                Supplier produces a value without receiving an input.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>import java.util.function.Supplier;

Supplier&lt;String&gt; message =
    () -&gt; "Hello Java";

System.out.println(
    message.get()
);</code></pre>
            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>Hello Java
</code></pre>
</div>

        </section>


        <!-- UNARY OPERATOR -->
        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                UnaryOperator&lt;T&gt;
            </h3>

            <p>
                UnaryOperator represents a function where the input and output
                are of the same type.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>import java.util.function.UnaryOperator;

UnaryOperator&lt;Integer&gt; square =
    number -&gt; number * number;

System.out.println(
    square.apply(5)
);</code></pre>
            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>


<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>25
</code></pre>
</div>
        </section>


        <!-- BINARY OPERATOR -->
        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                BinaryOperator&lt;T&gt;
            </h3>

            <p>
                BinaryOperator accepts two values of the same type and returns
                a value of that same type.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>import java.util.function.BinaryOperator;

BinaryOperator&lt;Integer&gt; addition =
    (a, b) -&gt; a + b;

System.out.println(
    addition.apply(10, 20)
);</code></pre>
            </div>
             <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>


<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>30
</code></pre>
</div>
        </section>


        <!-- BIFUNCTION -->
        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                BiFunction&lt;T,U,R&gt;
            </h3>

            <p>
                BiFunction accepts two inputs and produces one output.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>import java.util.function.BiFunction;

BiFunction&lt;Integer, Integer, Integer&gt; multiply =
    (a, b) -&gt; a * b;

System.out.println(
    multiply.apply(5, 4)
);</code></pre>
            </div>
          <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>20
</code></pre>
</div>

        </section>


        <!-- BIPREDICATE -->
        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                BiPredicate&lt;T,U&gt;
            </h3>

            <p>
                BiPredicate accepts two inputs and returns a boolean result.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>import java.util.function.BiPredicate;

BiPredicate&lt;Integer, Integer&gt; greater =
    (a, b) -&gt; a &gt; b;

System.out.println(
    greater.test(20, 10)
);</code></pre>
            </div>
             <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>


<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>true
</code></pre>
</div>
        </section>


        <!-- BICONSUMER -->
        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                BiConsumer&lt;T,U&gt;
            </h3>

            <p>
                BiConsumer accepts two inputs and does not return a value.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>import java.util.function.BiConsumer;

BiConsumer&lt;String, Integer&gt; display =
    (name, age) -&gt;
        System.out.println(
            name + " : " + age
        );

display.accept(
    "Amit",
    22
);</code></pre>
            </div>
             <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>


<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>Amit:22
</code></pre>
</div>
        </section>


        <!-- CUSTOM INTERFACE -->
        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Creating Your Own Functional Interface
            </h3>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>@@FunctionalInterface
interface MessageFormatter {

    String format(String message);

}

MessageFormatter formatter =
    message -&gt;
        "[INFO] " + message;

System.out.println(
    formatter.format(
        "Application started"
    )
);</code></pre>
            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>[INFO] Application started
</code></pre>
</div>
        </section>


        <!-- DEFAULT METHOD -->
        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Functional Interface with Default Method
            </h3>

            <p>
                A functional interface may contain default methods because
                default methods are not abstract methods.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>@@FunctionalInterface
interface Greeting {

    void sayHello();

    default void welcome() {

        System.out.println(
            "Welcome!"
        );

    }

}</code></pre>
            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- STATIC METHOD -->
        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Functional Interface with Static Method
            </h3>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>@@FunctionalInterface
interface Calculator {

    int calculate(int a, int b);

    static int square(int number) {

        return number * number;

    }

}</code></pre>
            </div>

            <p class="mt-3">
                Static methods belong to the interface itself and do not count
                as abstract methods.
            </p>

        </section>


        <!-- OBJECT METHODS -->
        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Object Methods and Functional Interfaces
            </h3>

            <p>
                Methods that correspond to public methods of
                <code>Object</code> do not count toward the single abstract
                method requirement.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>@@FunctionalInterface
interface Task {

    void execute();

    String toString();

}</code></pre>
            </div>

            <p class="mt-3">
                The interface still qualifies as functional because
                <code>toString()</code> corresponds to a method from
                <code>Object</code>.
            </p>

        </section>


        <!-- METHOD REFERENCE -->
        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Functional Interfaces and Method References
            </h3>

            <p>
                Method references can be assigned to functional interface
                variables when their signatures are compatible.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>Consumer&lt;String&gt; printer =
    System.out::println;

printer.accept(
    "Hello from Java"
);</code></pre>
            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>Hello from Java
</code></pre>
</div>
        </section>


        <!-- COLLECTIONS -->
        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Functional Interfaces with Collections
            </h3>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>List&lt;String&gt; names =
    List.of(
        "Amit",
        "Neha",
        "Rahul"
    );

names.forEach(
    name -&gt;
        System.out.println(name)
);</code></pre>
            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>Amit
Neha
Rahul</code></pre>
</div>

            <p class="mt-3">
                The <code>forEach()</code> operation accepts a
                <code>Consumer</code>.
            </p>

        </section>


        <!-- STREAM API -->
        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Functional Interfaces and Streams
            </h3>

            <p>
                Stream operations frequently accept functional interfaces.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>List&lt;Integer&gt; numbers =
    List.of(
        10,
        15,
        20,
        25,
        30
    );

numbers.stream()
    .filter(
        number -&gt; number &gt; 20
    )
    .map(
        number -&gt; number * 2
    )
    .forEach(
        System.out::println
    );</code></pre>
            </div>

            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>50
60</code></pre>
</div>
            <div class="alert alert-success mt-3">

                <strong>Key idea:</strong>

                <code>filter()</code> uses a predicate-like condition,
                <code>map()</code> transforms values, and
                <code>forEach()</code> consumes values.

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- COMPOSITION -->
        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Composing Functional Operations
            </h3>

            <p>
                Some functional interfaces provide methods that allow
                operations to be combined.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>Predicate&lt;Integer&gt; positive =
    number -&gt; number &gt; 0;

Predicate&lt;Integer&gt; even =
    number -&gt; number % 2 == 0;

Predicate&lt;Integer&gt; positiveAndEven =
    positive.and(even);

System.out.println(
    positiveAndEven.test(10)
);</code></pre>
            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>true
</code></pre>
</div>
        </section>


        <!-- FUNCTION COMPOSITION -->
        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Function Composition
            </h3>

            <p>
                Function interfaces support operations such as
                <code>andThen()</code> and <code>compose()</code>.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>Function&lt;Integer, Integer&gt; multiply =
    number -&gt; number * 2;

Function&lt;Integer, Integer&gt; add =
    number -&gt; number + 10;

Function&lt;Integer, Integer&gt; combined =
    multiply.andThen(add);

System.out.println(
    combined.apply(5)
);</code></pre>
            </div>

            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>20
</code></pre>
</div>
        </section>


        <!-- PRIMITIVE SPECIALIZATIONS -->
        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Primitive Functional Interfaces
            </h3>

            <p>
                Java also provides primitive-specialized functional interfaces
                to reduce unnecessary boxing and unboxing in suitable cases.
            </p>

            <div class="table-responsive">

                <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                    <thead class="table-dark">

                        <tr>
                            <th>Interface</th>
                            <th>Purpose</th>
                        </tr>

                    </thead>

                    <tbody>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>IntPredicate</code></td>
                            <td>Predicate for int values.</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>IntFunction&lt;R&gt;</code></td>
                            <td>Accepts int and returns a reference type.</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>IntConsumer</code></td>
                            <td>Consumes an int value.</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>IntSupplier</code></td>
                            <td>Supplies an int value.</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>IntUnaryOperator</code></td>
                            <td>Transforms one int into another int.</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>IntBinaryOperator</code></td>
                            <td>Combines two int values.</td>
                        </tr>

                    </tbody>

                </table>

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- REAL WORLD -->
        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Example
            </h3>

            <p>
                Functional interfaces are useful when business logic needs to
                be supplied dynamically.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>static int calculate(
    int a,
    int b,
    BinaryOperator&lt;Integer&gt; operation
) {

    return operation.apply(a, b);

}

int addition =
    calculate(
        10,
        20,
        (a, b) -&gt; a + b
    );

int multiplication =
    calculate(
        10,
        20,
        (a, b) -&gt; a * b
    );

System.out.println(addition);
System.out.println(multiplication);</code></pre>
            </div>

            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>30
200</code></pre>
</div>
        </section>


        <!-- BENEFITS -->
        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Benefits of Functional Interfaces
            </h3>

            <ul>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Enables lambda expressions.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Reduces boilerplate code.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Supports functional-style programming.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Works naturally with the Stream API.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Makes behavior easy to pass as an argument.
                </li>

                <li>
                    Encourages reusable and composable operations.
                </li>

            </ul>

        </section>


        <!-- BEST PRACTICES -->
        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Best Practices
            </h3>

            <ul>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Use <code>@@FunctionalInterface</code> when defining an
                    interface intended to be functional.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Prefer standard interfaces from
                    <code>java.util.function</code> when they fit the use case.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Create custom functional interfaces when domain-specific
                    meaning improves readability.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Keep lambda expressions short and readable.
                </li>

                <li>
                    Prefer method references when they make the code clearer.
                </li>

            </ul>

        </section>


        <!-- INTERVIEW QUESTIONS -->
        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Interview Questions
            </h3>

            <div class="accordion" id="functionalInterfaceQuestions">

                <!-- QUESTION 1 -->
                <div class="accordion-item">

                    <h2 class="accordion-header">

                        <button class="accordion-button collapsed"
                                type="button"
                                data-bs-toggle="collapse"
                                data-bs-target="#functionalQuestionOne">

                            What is a functional interface?

                        </button>

                    </h2>

                    <div id="functionalQuestionOne"
                         class="accordion-collapse collapse"
                         data-bs-parent="#functionalInterfaceQuestions">

                        <div class="accordion-body">

                            A functional interface is an interface with exactly
                            one abstract method.

                        </div>

                    </div>

                </div>


                <!-- QUESTION 2 -->
                <div class="accordion-item">

                    <h2 class="accordion-header">

                        <button class="accordion-button collapsed"
                                type="button"
                                data-bs-toggle="collapse"
                                data-bs-target="#functionalQuestionTwo">

                            What is @@FunctionalInterface?

                        </button>

                    </h2>

                    <div id="functionalQuestionTwo"
                         class="accordion-collapse collapse"
                         data-bs-parent="#functionalInterfaceQuestions">

                        <div class="accordion-body">

                            <code>@@FunctionalInterface</code> is an annotation
                            that documents and compiler-checks the intent that
                            an interface should contain one abstract method.

                        </div>

                    </div>

                </div>


                <!-- QUESTION 3 -->
                <div class="accordion-item">

                    <h2 class="accordion-header">

                        <button class="accordion-button collapsed"
                                type="button"
                                data-bs-toggle="collapse"
                                data-bs-target="#functionalQuestionThree">

                            Can a functional interface have default methods?

                        </button>

                    </h2>

                    <div id="functionalQuestionThree"
                         class="accordion-collapse collapse"
                         data-bs-parent="#functionalInterfaceQuestions">

                        <div class="accordion-body">

                            Yes. A functional interface can have multiple
                            default and static methods as long as it has only
                            one abstract method.

                        </div>

                    </div>

                </div>


                <!-- QUESTION 4 -->
                <div class="accordion-item">

                    <h2 class="accordion-header">

                        <button class="accordion-button collapsed"
                                type="button"
                                data-bs-toggle="collapse"
                                data-bs-target="#functionalQuestionFour">

                            What is Predicate?

                        </button>

                    </h2>

                    <div id="functionalQuestionFour"
                         class="accordion-collapse collapse"
                         data-bs-parent="#functionalInterfaceQuestions">

                        <div class="accordion-body">

                            <code>Predicate&lt;T&gt;</code> represents a
                            function that accepts a value and returns a
                            boolean result.

                        </div>

                    </div>

                </div>


                <!-- QUESTION 5 -->
                <div class="accordion-item">

                    <h2 class="accordion-header">

                        <button class="accordion-button collapsed"
                                type="button"
                                data-bs-toggle="collapse"
                                data-bs-target="#functionalQuestionFive">

                            What is the difference between Function and Consumer?

                        </button>

                    </h2>

                    <div id="functionalQuestionFive"
                         class="accordion-collapse collapse"
                         data-bs-parent="#functionalInterfaceQuestions">

                        <div class="accordion-body">

                            Function accepts an input and returns an output,
                            while Consumer accepts an input and returns
                            nothing.

                        </div>

                    </div>

                </div>


                <!-- QUESTION 6 -->
                <div class="accordion-item">

                    <h2 class="accordion-header">

                        <button class="accordion-button collapsed"
                                type="button"
                                data-bs-toggle="collapse"
                                data-bs-target="#functionalQuestionSix">

                            What is the difference between Predicate and Function?

                        </button>

                    </h2>

                    <div id="functionalQuestionSix"
                         class="accordion-collapse collapse"
                         data-bs-parent="#functionalInterfaceQuestions">

                        <div class="accordion-body">

                            Predicate returns a boolean result, while Function
                            transforms an input value into an output value.

                        </div>

                    </div>

                </div>


                <!-- QUESTION 7 -->
                <div class="accordion-item">

                    <h2 class="accordion-header">

                        <button class="accordion-button collapsed"
                                type="button"
                                data-bs-toggle="collapse"
                                data-bs-target="#functionalQuestionSeven">

                            Can lambda expressions be used without functional interfaces?

                        </button>

                    </h2>

                    <div id="functionalQuestionSeven"
                         class="accordion-collapse collapse"
                         data-bs-parent="#functionalInterfaceQuestions">

                        <div class="accordion-body">

                            A lambda expression needs a target functional
                            interface type, either explicitly or through
                            type inference.

                        </div>

                    </div>

                </div>

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- SUMMARY -->
        <section class="mb-4">

            <div class="alert alert-primary border-0">

                <h5 class="fw-bold">
                    Summary
                </h5>

                <p class="mb-0">
                    Functional interfaces contain one abstract method and form
                    the foundation for lambda expressions and method
                    references. Java provides Predicate, Function, Consumer,
                    Supplier, UnaryOperator, BinaryOperator, BiFunction,
                    BiPredicate, BiConsumer, and primitive-specialized
                    interfaces for common functional programming tasks.
                </p>

            </div>

        </section>


